A||||18||kslish||New daily driver? A Mercury Mystique?||||1||
Z||000000||kslish||09-07-2001||10:17 AM||||My winter beater Oldsmobile Cutlass Supreme is starting to tick me off with all the stuff that needs done with it (just turned 120,000 miles...was rock solid reliable until about 115,000).  Now stuff is starting to go on a monthly basis anymore.  <P>So basically I've started looking for a replacement....gotta be fun to drive (my Cutlass has the stiff FE3 Sport suspension), sorta unique, affordable and cheap to maintain.  <P>I just came across a nice jet black 1999 Mercury Mystique Sport V6 with 19,000 miles on it at a local Chrysler dealer.  It's pretty nice and I'm going to go test drive it after work today.  I checked out Contour.org and it looks like these cars have a decent aftermarket and enthusiast following.  The dealership is asking around $11K for it.  Good price?  Anyone know much about them and any problems to look for?  It'll have to last me 5 years or so (since I've got so many Fieros it won't get that many miles put on it anually...probably around 8,000 a year)....just some trips when I need a larger trunk and winter use.  <P>Anyone have any insight?<P>Thanks,<BR>Ken S.||||reg||1
Z||000001||OcalaFiero||09-07-2001||10:22 AM||||My mother has one. Last time I was up visiting, the car was coming up to the end of lease, they were quoted about the sam price to buy it, and I guess the ywere going to do it. I've driven the car a few times and was really surprised at how well it handled and how quick it really felt. I'd say go for it, that's not a bad price for that car and the V6 does give it some pull.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroLisa||09-07-2001||10:41 AM||||You need a Mercury Tracer  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The question is not how fast or well handling it is- can you fit parts in it??? Its a necessity to be able to fit Fiero carpet in the trunk  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/fierolisa.gif">     <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>NC Tarheels Fieros Site</A><P>AIM: FieroLisa||||reg||1
Z||000003||OcalaFiero||09-07-2001||11:01 AM||||Actually, If the one he's looking at is optioned out correctly, it'll have split fold down rear seats, and the trunk itself is big enough to fit a replacement airdam for a BMW M3 into it. (Ask my wife why we needed that, better her mistake than mine).<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000004||loafer87gt||09-07-2001||11:39 AM||||If you are looking for a good reliable, fun to drive car, check out a 95-97 Nissan 200SX SE-R. I test drove a 95 model the other day and couldn't believe how much fun it was to drive. The SE-R model has a 145 HP/135  torque 2.0 four banger that moves the car from 0-60 in 7.2 seconds and does the quarter in 15.6 seconds. The styling is a little subtle, but I haven't seen one negative review of the car. BTW, with a new ECU from Jim Wolfe, they will safely rev to 7700 RPM!!! Check out <A HREF="http://www.se-r.net" TARGET=_blank>www.se-r.net</A>  for more info.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.se-r.net/about/200sx/scc/july97/july1.jpg">  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.se-r.net/about/200sx/scc/august97/august3.jpg">  <P>Edit: These cars go for around $7500-10000 canadian for a 96-96 model. I'd steer clear of the 98 model as the don't have the limited slip differential.<p>[This message has been edited by loafer87gt (edited 09-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||09-07-2001||11:48 AM||||I like the Contour. Wanted an SVT. Prolly would have bought one if they were still made. <BR>Did a front brake job on a Contour that belongs to a friend. Easiest brake job I ever did.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 09-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||kslish||09-07-2001||02:01 PM||||Those Nissan 200SX SE-R's are nice, but a little smaller than what I'm looking for.  If I was picking a Nissan product it would have to be at least an Altima in size.  Don't get me wrong, I'm not looking for a road barge, just a nice fun mid size car.  <P>I'm just having visions of slapping some Contour SVT parts on the Mystique and making myself an econo BMW M3-like sedan.  Tastefully done of course, nothing ricey.  <P>And yes Lisa, the Mystique has the folding seatbacks so carpeting will fit.  You know I just want to be like you and drive a Mercury  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Ken S.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||09-07-2001||02:14 PM||||What about the Nissan 240SX?  You can get a nice SE model (leather, power everything) for around 10 grand, and I would bet my life that it is more reliable then any Mercury.<P>Its not that fast though, the SE with a 5 speed is about 0-60 in 8 seconds.  BTW, its RWD ;-)||||reg||14
Z||000008||rogergarrison||09-07-2001||05:27 PM||||Contour and Mystique are same platform. Sort of a lower end Taurus/ Sable. Prob pretty reliable till they got some miles. I get a lot of used ones that are traded in and seem to run pretty decent up to 160k or so. Lots of plastic in the interior.||||reg||1
Z||000009||kslish||09-07-2001||06:03 PM||||Thanks Roger, that's the kind of stuff I'm looking for.  If it would last me for 100,000 miles I would be pretty happy.  I just got back from test driving it.  Turns out it also is pretty loaded with gray leather and stuff, but you're right...the dash does look rather cheap with all the plastic.  Nice driving car though..seats seemed a little too narrow to me though, but fortunately I'm not that wide.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  The engine goes very well when you rev it, but there isn't much lower end torque in around town driving.  Guess there is only so much you can expect out of a 2.5L even if it is a 24V DOHC engine.<P>This car purchase has to be a front wheel drive.  We just have too many hills and too much lake effect snow in the winter.  If that wasn't the case, I'd consider a RWD 240SX or something like that.<P>Now I just gotta figure out if I feel like spending the money and if the car is something I can live with for a few years.<P>Ken||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fformula88||09-07-2001||06:34 PM||||My only word of caution would be over which transmission is in that Mystique.  If its an automatic, chances are its Ford's CD4E unit which may be the worst transmission on the market.  They simply do not hold up well.  This unit has seen action in Contour/Mystiques, Escapes, Cougars and Probes and has been terrible for reliability.  They usually go bad between 30K-60K miles, requiring rebuilds or replacement.<P>My friend has a 96 Probe SE and the thing went bad at 32K miles.  My daily driver is a 97 Probe SE, and the tranny died at 11K miles.  I had owned the car for 9 months!  Thank god for warrenties!||||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula88||09-07-2001||07:02 PM||||Check out Hertz.com.  I purchased a 99 Grand Am used rental from them in March of 99.  It already had 24K miles on it, but I also got it for $13,500.  Not bad for a brand new car, basically.  I've got 70K miles on it now, and it's been a good car. Also, you can get the extended warranty from GM (don't let them sell you a Hertz warranty - get the GM), so you're covered, too.<P>BTW, that 3400SFI is a great little engine.  I've driven this car EVERYWHERE.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Raydar||09-07-2001||07:30 PM||||Now that you reminded me...<BR>My friend's Contour needed a tranny rebuild at about 90K. 24v V-6 and automatic. FWIW<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Cooter||09-07-2001||08:02 PM||||My wife had a '95 Mystique. It was not a great car, but not too bad as far as breakdowns and upkeep. Nothing major ever happened during the approx 100,000 miles she had it, until she totaled it and the air bag broke her arm.||||reg||1
Z||000014||artherd||09-07-2001||09:50 PM||||Find a pre-95 Nissan Maxima, those cars are great  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  bulletproof reliable, and will beat a Fiero to 60 (and to 120  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Very very nice with the 5-speed, but the engine might actually be a little powerful in snow conditions  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000015||frontal lobe||09-07-2001||11:18 PM||||Hey Ben, do you not like the '95 and later model change of the Maxima, or did you not recommend those years because they would exceed the $11,000 range?<P>I have driven the v6 5-speed mystique and I liked it.  My only question would be how often is someone going to wind up in the back seat, because to me it seemed pretty tight.<P>AND DON'T FLAME ME, but in that price range, the Chevy Malibu with the 3.1 isn't bad.  Lot's of room for it's size, decent acceleration; but only an automatic and I don't know about the reliability.  Plus, resale wouldn't be good if you don't plan to drive it until it dies.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Fformula88||09-07-2001||11:23 PM||||No flame intended frontal lobe, but the Malibu is one of the worst cars in reliability ratings that exist on the market!  I guess there is a reason why its so cheap, and yet you don't see too many on the road!||||reg||1
Z||000017||frontal lobe||09-07-2001||11:49 PM||||Bummer.  What is with GM, anyway?||||reg||1
Z||000018||FieroLisa||09-08-2001||12:14 AM||||Wonder if thats the same tranny in my Tracer? It needed rebuilt round 75K miles..wasn't too happy with that bill.||||reg||1
